temper (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Access to the stick moves to the temper package: its udev rule
    gives the stick's data interface to group temper and to the user
    at the seat, so temper runs without sudo, and services that read
    the stick directly (such as smartclock-sensord) join group temper.
    Before, the rule was temper-iio's, and temper alone left the stick
    root-only.
  * temper-iio now depends on temper and runs with group temper; its
    own rule only starts the unit.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 02:10:25 -0700

temper (1.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * On Linux, a stick in use is locked: temper and temper-iio each hold
    an exclusive lock on the stick's hidraw node while they have it
    open, and a second one says the stick is in use instead of reading
    the other's replies (temper read beside the daemon could fail with
    "firmware string is not printable ASCII").  In the library, this is
    the new hid::Error::Busy.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 01:57:46 -0700

temper (1.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Windows: the temper-hid library and the temper tool now run on
    Windows, through hidapi's pure-Rust Windows backend.  Each release
    carries temper-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.exe.  Built and tested on
    Windows in CI, but not yet tried with a stick there: opening the
    data interface while Windows holds the stick's keyboard interface,
    the timing, and the errors an unplug gives are unverified.
  * No change on Linux.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 01:42:19 -0700

temper (1.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First release as temper, renamed from tempered (history at
    https://github.com/charlieh0tel/tempered-hid).  Two packages:
    temper, the command-line tool (temper read, info, log), and
    temper-iio, the daemon that presents a TEMPerGold or TEMPerHUM as
    Linux IIO devices.
  * The daemon's unit, user, settings and links are named temper-iio:
    temper-iio@, /etc/default/temper-iio with TEMPER_IIO_* settings,
    /run/temper-iio.
  * On crates.io: temper-hid, the library (was tempered-hid; API
    hid::Device, Stick::reading, Celsius, RelativeHumidityPercent,
    schedule::Schedule), and temper-hid-cli, the temper tool.
  * Linux only for now; Windows support is planned.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 23:12:45 -0700
